From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: dp: register a drm_bridge -- the DP half of Stage 2

[Recipe cut: regenerated: the probe message no longer points at the removed
aux_enable file.]

The planned architecture for Stage 2 makes the DP a drm_bridge and an
encoder component, modelled on dw_dp-rockchip.c (150 lines) and
sun8i_dw_hdmi.c (302 lines). sun60i-dp.c keeps its debugfs verbs -- the tree's
diagnostics rule says those lines are never deleted -- and gains
drm_bridge_add(), .attach (with drm_dp_aux_register() moved there), .detect,
.edid_read, .mode_valid and .atomic_enable/disable around the link training.

This is that bridge, and NOTHING ELSE. It is the half that can be written
without a drm_device: the encoder needs one (drmm_encoder_init() takes it) and
so does the connector, and this tree has no component master yet. So the bridge
is published with drm_bridge_add() -- the mechanism whose whole purpose is a
bridge that exists BEFORE the encoder that will attach it -- and the KMS half
finds it with of_drm_find_bridge()/devm_drm_of_get_bridge() on our of_node.

WHAT RUNS TODAY, EXACTLY
-------------------------

One list insertion at probe. Nothing in this tree creates an encoder, so nothing
calls drm_bridge_attach(), so .attach, .detach, .detect, .edid_read,
.mode_valid, .hpd_notify, .atomic_enable and .atomic_disable HAVE NEVER
EXECUTED -- not on hardware, not anywhere. Everything that worked before this
patch still works through the same debugfs verbs (aux_enable, dpcd, edid, link,
train, video), which are untouched.

That is also why the vendor-comparison table below is short on measurements and
long on structure: this patch adds shape, not register content.

HOW THE VENDOR STACK DOES IT, AND WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD
--------------------------------------------------------

This table covers the connect/detect path specifically. The
AUX, link-rate and training rows are already answered by `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux`, `drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy` and `drm-a733-dp-link-training` and
are not repeated.

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| "detect" is a POLL: edp_sink_extcon_evt() reschedules drm_edp_typec_hpd_monitor() once a second for up to 30 rounds, each round issuing DPCD reads into a front end whose bias is off -- measured on the 8 GiB board with nothing in Type-C J4: eDP binds at 1.646 s and logs 34 "edp wait aux reply timeout!" between 1.69 s and 6.21 s, every boot | .detect reads NO register and issues NO transaction. It returns what .hpd_notify recorded. The channel is opened once, when the alternate mode says a sink is there |
| that poll runs whether or not the mux is in a DP state, so "no sink" is expressed as a stream of error-level timeouts | "no sink" is a state: .detect returns connector_status_disconnected and logs nothing, on every one of the ten-second polls the DRM core will run for ever |
| the sink's presence is inferred from an extcon the driver polls | the alternate mode's own HPD, delivered by drm_connector_oob_hotplug_event() from typec_displayport.ko -- the path drm_connector.c's kerneldoc describes as being for "some USB Type-C setups where the hardware muxes the DisplayPort data and aux-lines but does not pass the altmode HPD status bit to the GPU's DP HPD pin", which is this board |
| ships hdmi-toggle-once.service ENABLED: sleep 15, wait 20 s for SDDM's X cookie, switch to the second EDID mode and back, once per boot behind a lock file -- a timing workaround in userspace for a modeset that does not come up right | a modeset that does not need one; if ours ever does, that is a driver bug with a log line |
| its eDP driver takes phy_power_on() and phy_configure() on a combo PHY it leaves to "other module" to initialise (sunxi_drm_edp.c:826-843) | unchanged from `drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy`: phy_init()/phy_exit() taken by the consumer, refcounted |

THE THREE DECISIONS THAT ARE NOT STYLE
---------------------------------------

1. NO DRM_BRIDGE_OP_HPD. The op means "this bridge detects hot-plug and calls
   drm_bridge_hpd_notify()". This one cannot. HPD_INPUT_STATE, eDP0 offset
   0x0128, absolute 0x05740128, reads 0x00000000 on this board while a picture
   is on the wire -- read on 2026-08-19 against the VENDOR's live link, so it is
   the board and not our bring-up (the pad is not
   routed to the connector). Claiming the op would give the connector
   DRM_CONNECTOR_POLL_HPD, which waits for a notification that can never arrive:
   a display that never returns after a re-plug. DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DETECT|_EDID
   instead, which makes the connector POLL_CONNECT|POLL_DISCONNECT
   (drm_bridge_connector.c:845-848) -- and that is affordable only because
   .detect touches nothing.

2. THE AUX TRANSFER PATH GAINS A GUARD, AND WITHOUT IT THIS PATCH IS A
   REGRESSION. `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` deliberately did not create the /dev/drm_dp_auxN chardev --
   "the part that needs the DRM parent" -- so until now every route into
   sun60i_dp_aux_transfer() came through a debugfs verb or through an i2c
   adapter that exists only while the block is enabled. drm_dp_aux_register()
   creates both a chardev any process can drive and an adapter that survives
   `echo 0 > aux_enable`. Both would land in the transfer function with the bus
   clock gated and the reset asserted -- the state in which `drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` MEASURED the
   whole 4 KiB window reading zero -- and produce a 3 ms poll ending in a
   timeout that says nothing about the sink. `if (!dp->enabled) return -ENODEV;`
   is the answer, and -ENODEV rather than -ETIMEDOUT because the core retries a
   timeout and cannot retry its way to a different answer here.

3. ONE PIXEL CLOCK IN .mode_valid, and the restriction is honest rather than
   conservative. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`'s own header names what it owes: "a SECOND mode. Nothing
   has ever asked this PLL for one" -- a mode whose pixel clock is not an
   integer divisor of a reachable VCO lands on the nearest lower rate, and the
   falsifier it names is SRC0_MVID not computing back to the requested clock.
   Until that measurement exists, a connector with an EDID mode list would hand
   this driver clocks nothing has ever produced, with only `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source`'s 1 % readback
   between that and a silently wrong raster. So the mode list is gated on the
   one clock this tree has driven. It sits in the bridge only because the CRTC
   does not exist yet; it belongs to whoever owns clk_round_rate() on the pixel
   clock, which is TCON_TV1.

WHAT IS REUSED, NAMED
----------------------

  drm_bridge_add / devm_drm_bridge_add / devm_drm_bridge_alloc  drm_bridge.c
  drm_atomic_helper_bridge_{reset,duplicate_state,destroy_state}
                                                  drm_atomic_state_helper.c
  drm_atomic_helper_bridge_propagate_bus_fmt      drm_atomic_helper.c
  drm_dp_aux_register / _unregister               drm_dp_helper.c
  drm_dp_max_dprx_data_rate                       drm_dp_helper.c
  drm_edid_read_ddc                               drm_edid.c
  drm_atomic_get_new_connector_for_encoder        drm_atomic.c

Genuinely A733: which ops may be claimed (the HPD measurement above), the
link-budget question being asked of phy_validate() rather than of the sink, the
ddc-ownership handover, and the AUX guard.

Kconfig gains `select DRM_KMS_HELPER`, because the four atomic bridge-state
helpers live in drm_kms_helper.ko (drivers/gpu/drm/Makefile: drm_kms_helper-y +=
drm_atomic_state_helper.o). It is already =m on this board -- but only because
DRM_SUN4I selects it, a driver that binds nothing here and that any cleanup
deletes. That is the same kind of accident that once held up
the console chain, and it is not relied on twice. drm_kms_helper.ko is already
in A733_MODULE_FILES, so this adds no packing debt.

VERIFIED
---------

  applies clean : yes, `patch -p1 --fuzz=0`
  builds        : yes -- drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ compiles with no warning,
                  native aarch64 gcc 14.2.0, a733_defconfig-configured tree.
                  Every new undefined symbol checked against an EXPORT_SYMBOL
                  in a module this config builds.
  on silicon    : NO. Not hardware-proven, and stronger than that: not
                  hardware-EXERCISED. See "what runs today" above.

⚠ Applies clean is not the same claim as composes with the parallel display
work. Checked separately: this patch also applies together with
`drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge` and `drm-a733-dp-link-training` of this series, and that union compiles. It edits no file the display
engine edits.

OPEN, EACH AS ONE OBSERVABLE
-----------------------------

1. THE OUT-OF-BAND HPD MAY NOT REACH THE CONNECTOR, and this patch cannot fix
   it. drm_connector_find_by_fwnode() matches connector->fwnode, which
   drm_bridge_connector sets from the LAST bridge's of_node -- our DP node --
   while dp_altmode_probe() takes the Type-C port's own fwnode unless that port
   carries a `displayport` property naming another (displayport.c:800-804). Two
   different nodes, so the event would be dropped. That property is read by the
   code and documented in NO binding: usb-connector.yaml is
   `unevaluatedProperties: false` and documents only an `altmodes/displayport`
   CHILD NODE, which is not a property and does not satisfy the lookup; no DT in
   6.18.36 uses it. Observable, once a connector exists: plug the monitor and
   watch /sys/class/drm/<card>-DP-1/status. If it does not change, the fwnode
   did not match. The fix is a DT edit plus a binding, and it belongs with the
   connector, not here.

2. .edid_read MAY RETIRE the earlier two-block EDID reader's CAP. The earlier two-block EDID reader (not carried in this recipe) reads at most two blocks
   because this core's AUX sends a one-byte offset, and its header records the
   E-DDC segment pointer as arriving "with the display stack instead of being
   hand-rolled now". drm_edid_read_ddc() writes that pointer itself. Untested:
   the bench sink declares exactly one extension block and cannot tell the two
   apart. Observable: a sink with three or more blocks, compared against what
   the `edid` debugfs file reports it skipped.

3. THE TWO-DRIVERS-ONE-MODE INVARIANT IS NOT CLOSED. sun60i-display.h exists
   because TCON_TV1 and this transmitter must describe the SAME mode; the
   hardware reports a disagreement as SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS != 0, flagging a
   mismatch with the current video mode. From here the transmitter
   takes its mode from the atomic state -- the retirement path that header names
   -- while the TCON still takes the constant until it gains a CRTC, and `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1`
   takes it too. Observable: SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS non-zero on the first modeset
   where the two disagree; the driver already reads and prints it.

4. NOTHING ATTACHES THE BRIDGE. Observable that closes it: `dmesg` showing this
   driver's .attach message on a boot where a component master built a
   drm_device, and /sys/class/drm/ carrying a DP connector.

Assisted-by: Claude

